{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017awBb3HUidVMJdenEPUSxr","type":"tool_result","content":"../skill/scripts/review_coverage.py ledger ../agent/brace-findings.json A01.1 -> 0 Saved ledger for A01.1; run the validator before retaining yes.\n../skill/scripts/answer.py ../agent/brace-findings.json check A01.1 -> 0 Saved A01.1 = yes. Next: A01.2 — Tool and API clients use short-lived credentials with a verified acting-agent identity and any separately bounded user/system delegation; they do not copy a login session or reuse a gateway token at a different backend audience.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace every client credential to its issuer, audience, acting principal and delegated subject. 2) Accept authorized OAuth delegation and supported exchange when agent identity and bounded grants survive. 3) Reject copied sessions, shared static keys, or cross-audience token reuse. 4) Mark issuer/downstream guarantees\n../skill/scripts/answer.py ../agent/brace-findings.json check A01.2 -> 0 Saved A01.2 = no. Next: A01.3 — For user-initiated runs, the original user is a verified delegated subject while the acting agent retains its own principal.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace user-started runs and credential issuance, including OAuth delegation and token exchange where supported. 2) Check original subject, acting agent and granted task/tenant/resource limits independently. 3) Reject a user label from model-controlled input or replacement of agent identity with a human session. 4) For machine-only operation, cite the configuration showing there is no delegated user; do not invent one.\n../skill/scripts/answer.py ../agent/brace-findings.json check A01.3 -> 0 Saved A01.3 = no. Next: A01.4 — Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n  How to verify: 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they